module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.interpreted_recursion_depth_is_host_stack

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { CeilingNotStated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data interpreted_recursion_depth_is_host_stack: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "interpreted_recursion_depth_is_host_stack" as NonEmptyStr,

  // FOUND AT rung 1, mitigatable per site: an author rewrites one recursion to run through the
  // intercepted fold and that site stops consuming host stack (three sites were, in gunbc#15258). The
  // CEILING is deliberately not stated here because it is not this row's to state. §4b asks a
  // below-ceiling class to name its next-rung trigger, and the trigger is named in the last receipt:
  // the native-execution / interpreter-retirement capability. Until that capability lands there is no
  // rung to stand on, so the row is filed unread on the ceiling rather than guessing one.
  rung: RungUnread { cause: CeilingNotStated },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a `.dag` function whose recursion count grows with the size of its input runs on
     the v1 interpreter, and the interpreter gives every interpreted call a host frame. Call depth is
     therefore host stack depth. Nothing in the source says so: the function parses, type-checks, and
     is correct on every small input, and it fails only when some consumer hands it a big one. The
     failure can also arrive as someone else's death, because the recursion is a property of the
     CALLEE's shape while the frame belongs to whoever's witness happens to be running.",

    "WHY IT LOOKS SAFE AND ISN'T, and this is the instructive part. Tail position is not a mitigation
     HERE. The v1 interpreter performs NO tail-call optimization: `tco`, `tail call` and `trampoline`
     appear nowhere in src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs, and the only interpreted functions the
     interpreter realizes natively at all are fold_list and fold_list_right (its
     `native_intercept.fold_list`, `native_intercept.fold_list_right` arms). `__tco_loop` -- the marker
     that makes people believe the recursion is handled -- is produced by the EMITTER
     (is_tco_eligible / emit_shared_tco_expr in src/v1/05_emit.dag) and exists only in generated Rust.
     So a function can carry a __tco_loop in its mirror, be genuinely O(1) on the native lane, and
     still put one host frame per element on the wet lane, which is what `claim_batch --claim-run`
     executes. Re-derive at any revision: grep the interpreter for the three words and grep the emitted
     mirror for __tco_loop, and read the two facts side by side. Checking the emitted Rust proves the
     native lane; it says nothing about the interpreted one.",

    "TWO REPORTING SURFACES FOR THE ONE CAUSE, which is why this class reads as two unrelated bugs.
     Below the interpreter's own call-depth ceiling the recursion is invisible. At the ceiling the wet
     lane refuses LOUDLY with a typed runtime error that names the function and says `refused, never a
     host stack overflow`. Past it -- on a path the ceiling does not intercept -- the process dies
     SILENTLY by host stack overflow, naming nothing, and the claim that dies is whatever witness was
     running rather than the function at fault. Re-derive both: run a wet claim over an input long
     enough to be deep, once through a function the ceiling sees and once where it does not
     (`claim_batch --wet --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry <file> --functions <name>
     --claim-run`). Both surfaces were seen on two shapes of this one defect in gunbc#15258.",

    "HOW A FAULTY FRAME IS FOUND, since the silent surface names nothing. A backtrace of a wet-lane
     abort shows only interpreter frames (call_function -> call_function_dispatch -> call_function_inner
     -> eval_expr -> eval_pure_named_call): it identifies the interpreter, never the `.dag` function.
     The instrument that names it is a call-depth trace over the interpreter's own dispatch
     (src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs call_function_dispatch, whose `fn_node.name` IS the `.dag`
     symbol), printing the callee whenever host call depth sets a new maximum. The interpreter carries
     no such hook, so whoever needs the name adds the print -- it is a diagnostic, not a shipped
     instrument, which is why the census below asks for a real one. THE SIGNATURE to read off it: depth
     climbing by a FIXED STEP per input element. A step greater than one means a MUTUAL family -- the
     located case climbed three per sibling, through a pair comparator calling two node comparison
     functions that called it back -- and single-function scanning therefore under-reports this class
     structurally. `tail_position` alone also misreads the cost in this lane: what holds a frame is a
     call whose result is still needed (inside `&&` or `||`, a constructor field, an argument, a
     comparison) and, for want of any tail-call optimization, a pure self-tail loop as well. That last
     is what makes the class wide: tail-recursive walkers written in the good style are still charged.",

    "THE POPULATION IS WIDE AND IS NOT COUNTED HERE. Any self- or mutually-recursive `.dag` function
     whose recursion count grows with an input is in this class, across both governed trees, and the
     pure-self-tail style is in it too. No count is recorded: the scan that produced the first estimate
     was a one-off with no entry point in the tree, so under §6 it is not an instrument and its number
     would rot unreachably. What would make the population countable is a census with a producer -- a
     `gunbc test` label over both trees classifying each call site by whether the enclosing expression
     still needs the result -- and that is the follow-up this row explicitly does not undertake.
     Per-site rewrites are rung 1 work and were taken only where a named consumer was blocked, not
     across the class.",

    "WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. No site is proven reachable with an input large enough to die -- most are
     never called with anything long. The finding is about the COST MODEL: depth is unpriced and charged
     to a shared host stack, so the failure is deferred to whichever consumer crosses the line, and the
     two surfaces above make it unattributable when it does. A site with no large-input consumer is not
     a defect waiting to be fixed; it is the reason the class goes unnoticed.",

    "CEILING AND TRIGGER. The per-site mitigation exists and works: run the walk through the
     intercepted fold (the interpreter's native_intercept.fold_list / native_intercept.fold_list_right
     arms, emitted as a __tco_loop). The class retires only under the native-execution /
     interpreter-retirement capability, and that capability IS the next-rung trigger: when the wet lane
     stops being an execution surface, depth stops being charged to a host stack. Whoever owns that
     capability owns this row's ceiling; the row does not guess it.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "std.algebra", decl_name: "zip_eq"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "std.algebra", decl_name: "list_init"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v2.extdeps.languages.dag", decl_name: "dag_string_framed_body_optional"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v2.test.claim.algebra.list_linear_combinators", decl_name: "zip_eq_compares_a_list_too_long_to_recurse_over"),
  ],
}
